    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the goblin misstep. Let's get down to the brass tacks of pallet stacking and center of gravity.\n\nWhen stacking pallets, the center of gravity is crucial for maintaining the stability of the entire structure. If you place heavier pallets on top of lighter ones, the center of gravity will shift upwards. This upward shift can increase the risk of the stack becoming top-heavy and more susceptible to tipping over, especially if the load is uneven or if there are external forces like wind or movement in the warehouse.\n\nTo maintain structural integrity, it's generally recommended to stack pallets in a way that the center of gravity remains as low as possible. This means placing the heaviest pallets at the bottom and the lighter ones on top. By doing so, you create a stable base that can support the weight of the upper pallets without compromising the overall stability of the rack.\n\nAdditionally, when stacking pallets, it's important to consider the interlocking patterns. Interlocking patterns can help distribute the weight more evenly across the rack, reducing the risk of any single point of failure. This can be particularly important when dealing with high-density racking setups, as the weight of the loads can be substantial.\n\nIn summary, to maximize stability and structural integrity in your high-density racking",
